 Trade Center  in an apparent terrorist attack on our  country  preserving a president's records is a  big deal it's the law  and it's also important so that future  researchers Scholars historians  documentarians can get access to these  documents to document history to learn  from president's successes and mistakes  and to avoid those mistakes in the  future which is why it's a law that  every record that deals with the  president's work in office must be sent  to the archives but it's the president's  staff that is actually in charge of  doing that this is where things can get  messy the archives just offer guidance  they aren't checking these boxes leaving  the White House the challenge is people  in the White House work up until January  20th National Security issues don't stop  because there's a new president these  people are busy they're exhausted  they're trying to figure out their  future they're trying to do their  current job and you add to that the  burden of preserving all their documents  it's a lot a person familiar with the  classified documents found on premises  used by Biden told the Wall Street  Journal that one was related to a trip  to Ukraine he took a few days before his  vice presidential term ended it was just  one stop in a hectic travel itinerary  during the last two weeks before the  transition I'll be Vice President of the  United States for 48 more hours when a  president travels their staff goes with  them that means the staff's not in the  office to preserve the records and it  just adds to the challenge it adds to  the pressure and it reduces the amount  of time staff has to preserve their  records Trump's last days were also  chaotic not because of travel but  because he was in the middle of his  second impeachment AIDS at the time said  they delayed packing because of Trump's  attempts to overturn the election the  White House also typically sends records  to the archives throughout the  administration as they are done with  them